  "article": {
    "hero": {
      "title": "🎸 Band Name Generator",
      "tagline": "Band names by genre, built on the patterns real bands use."
    },
    "intro": "Most band names follow a handful of shapes: <em>The</em> and a plural (The Beatles, The Strokes), an adjective and a noun (Arctic Monkeys), a single word (Nirvana, Metallica) or something stranger. Genre changes the words you reach for, not the shapes. This **band name generator** builds names in all those shapes for six genres, so you can find one that sounds like your music before you check it's free.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Where famous band names came from",
        "content": "<ul><li><strong>The Beatles</strong>: a pun on beetles and the beat, a nod to Buddy Holly's Crickets.</li><li><strong>The Rolling Stones</strong>: from Muddy Waters' song \"Rollin' Stone\".</li><li><strong>Radiohead</strong>: from the Talking Heads song \"Radio Head\", after the band started out as On a Friday.</li><li><strong>Pink Floyd</strong>: from two blues musicians, Pink Anderson and Floyd Council.</li><li><strong>Black Sabbath</strong>: from the 1963 horror film of the same name, starring Boris Karloff.</li><li><strong>Led Zeppelin</strong>: from a joke that the band would go down like a lead balloon; the spelling was changed so people wouldn't say \"leed\".</li></ul><p>The pattern is worth noticing: a song, a joke, a film, a pun. Names that came from somewhere usually have more character than names made up to sound cool.</p>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "How genre changes the name",
        "content": "<ul><li><strong>Metal:</strong> dark, heavy and often ancient. Thrones, serpents, iron and frost.</li><li><strong>Punk:</strong> short, rude and funny. A punk name should annoy someone's parents.</li><li><strong>Indie:</strong> gentle and a little odd. Everyday objects, weather, small sad animals.</li><li><strong>Folk:</strong> landscape and work. Rivers, hedgerows, fiddlers and ploughmen.</li><li><strong>Electronic:</strong> light, signals and machines. Often one word, often invented.</li><li><strong>Rock:</strong> movement and noise. Engines, highways, wolves and hearts.</li></ul>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Check it's free before you print the T-shirts",
        "content": "<p>Thousands of bands share names, and finding out after your first release is painful. Before you settle on one:</p><ul><li><strong>Search the streaming services</strong> (Spotify, Apple Music) and <strong>Bandcamp</strong> for the exact name.</li><li><strong>Search Discogs and MusicBrainz</strong>, which list older and smaller releases streaming may miss.</li><li><strong>Check social handles and a domain</strong>, so fans can find you.</li><li><strong>Check trade marks</strong> if you're serious about it: the UK IPO, USPTO and EUIPO all have free searches. This isn't legal advice; if in doubt, ask someone who can give it.</li></ul><p>A small twist (a different article, an unusual spelling, an extra word) can make a taken name your own.</p>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Picking the one",
        "content": "<ul><li><strong>Say it out loud,</strong> as a gig announcement: \"Please welcome... The Paper Lanterns!\"</li><li><strong>Imagine it on a poster and a T-shirt.</strong> Long names shrink; short ones shout.</li><li><strong>Make it searchable.</strong> A common word on its own is hard to find online. Two words are easier.</li><li><strong>Live with it for a week.</strong> If you still like it, it's probably the one.</li></ul><p>For album names, try the <a href=\"album-name-generator.html\">Album Name Generator</a>; for something darker and more atmospheric, the <a href=\"dungeon-synth-name-generator.html\">Dungeon Synth Name Generator</a>.</p>"
      }
    ],
    "faqs": [
      {
        "question": "How do I check if a band name is taken?",
        "answer": "Search Spotify, Apple Music, Bandcamp, Discogs and MusicBrainz for the exact name, then check social handles and domains. For a serious project, search trade mark registers too (UK IPO, USPTO, EUIPO)."
      },
      {
        "question": "Should my band name start with \"The\"?",
        "answer": "It's a classic shape (The Beatles, The Strokes, The Cure) and works best with a plural noun. Many bands drop it later, so pick a name that works either way."
      },
      {
        "question": "Can I use these names for my band?",
        "answer": "Yes, free and with no credit needed, but check the name isn't already used by another band first."
      }
    ]
  }
